infographic hierarchy-mindmap-level-gradient-compact-card
data
  title Four ways TypeScript types the receiver
  desc One fake parameter for receiver safety, three return/marker tricks that model real APIs — all erased or inert at runtime
  root
    label this-typed features
    children
      - label this: T parameter
        desc Types the call-time receiver and is erased at emit. Under noImplicitThis (in strict) it surfaces the lost-this bug when a method is detached and called unbound.
      - label this is Derived guard
        desc A method returning this is Derived narrows the receiver itself in the calling scope — isDir() refines node to the subclass inside the if-branch.
      - label polymorphic this
        desc A method returning this means "the concrete current class", so fluent builders stay typed across subclasses — where() keeps subclass methods chainable.
      - label ThisType<T>
        desc A contextual-typing marker with no runtime effect that sets what this is inside object-literal method bags — the Vue-2 options object, mixins, fluent config.
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6 #f59e0b #8b5cf6
